<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Interpretatio romana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Interpretatio romana was the Roman practice of identifying foreign gods with Roman deities, such as equating a Celtic god with Mercury or Mars. It allowed local cults to be absorbed within Roman religion. The site cites this religious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syncretism">syncretism</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>'s cultural legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/interpretatio-romana.jpg" alt="Interpretatio romana" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WolfgangRieger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pompeii_-_Temple_of_Isis_-_Io_and_Isis_-_MAN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
